  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA 25-30kt 850mb jet streak oriented from the Texas panhandle\nnortheastward to Iowa continues to provide the necessary fuel\nfor repeated clusters of convection from eastern Iowa across\nnorthern Illinois early this morning. As expected, the bulk of\nthis activity is remaining north of the KILX CWA...with only\nlocations along/north of an Avon to El Paso line occasionally \nbeing grazed. While the heaviest rainfall will stay to the \nnorth, will need to keep an eye on a trailing line of \nshowers/thunder currently extending from southeast Iowa into \nnorth-central Missouri. 00z CAMs have not handled the precip \nparticularly well: however, based on radar trends, it appears at\nleast scattered convection will push into the Illinois River \nValley toward dawn and approach I-55 by mid-morning. Given \nthe current set-up, have focused 60-80 PoPs along/north of I-72\nthis morning. Convective re-development will likely occur this \nafternoon along the residual outflow boundary left behind by the\nmorning showers...with the most probable location of the\nboundary being south of I-72. While most CAMs are largely dry, \nthink scattered thunderstorms will develop within the marginally\nunstable/sheared environment during peak heating. 00z HREF \nshows ensemble mean SBCAPEs of just 1500J/kg, but 0-6km shear of\naround 30kt. While these parameters certainly do not point \ntoward widespread severe weather, think a few of the stronger \ncells may be capable of gusty 50-60mph wind gusts between 2pm \nand 8pm south of I-72. Areal coverage of the afternoon cells \nwill be low, with just 20-40 PoPs within the SPC Day 1 Marginal \nRisk area.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms will become a bit more numerous \nacross the southern half of the CWA tonight as a weak LLJ\ninteracts with the outflow/frontal boundary. Based on model\nconsensus, have focused highest PoPs of 50-60% south of I-72. \nWhile the persistent front responsible for the disturbed weather\nover the past few days drops south of central Illinois on Monday,\ntrailing upper-level energy will keep scattered showers in the \nforecast before gradually tapering off and coming to an end by \nTuesday.\n\nThe main weather story for the week ahead will be the significant\npattern shift to much cooler temperatures. Highs will drop into\nthe 60s everywhere north of the I-70 corridor Monday through\nWednesday before slowly moderating back into the 70s by the end\nof the week. The coolest night ahead still appears to be \nTuesday night when lows reach the lower to middle 50s. Even \ncooler readings in the upper 40s are possible in favored low-\nlying/rural spots as the 00z NBM indicates a 30-50% chance of \nlows dipping below 50 degrees along/west of I-57...and a 60-70% \nnorthwest of the Illinois River.",
